// `rtwasm`: the C library the runtime calls, written for the wasm32 target
// (docs/self-hosting.md §6.24, decision (6)).
//
// The runtime in Grasm (`compiler/rt`) is compiled for wasm as it is for
// macOS, the same TIR, and calls the C functions it calls there by the same
// names and with Darwin's numbers; the wasm link (`compiler/wasm32`) binds
// each call to the function of that name here, and refuses a name nothing
// defines. So this is a C library in the shape the runtime expects:
//
//     mem.gr    memory: an allocator over `memory.grow`, `memcpy` and its kin
//     math.gr   libm: Rust's `libm` crate's functions (musl's, FreeBSD's),
//               as the Rust playground's module has them
//     sys.gr    clocks, entropy, the way out, and what wasm cannot do --
//               files, sockets, processes, threads -- each failing with
//               ENOTSUP, which `strerror` words as the Rust playground's
//               does ("operation not supported on this platform")
//     streams.gr  the standard streams, through the host; a module built
//               `--exports` takes `play/streams.gr` in its place, which
//               keeps them in memory (docs/self-hosting.md §6.25)
//     stacks.gr the context switch over JS promise integration, for a
//               module built `--exports`
//     reset.gr  a playground run's beginning: the instance's first state
//
// The host is reached through imports only (`__host_<name>`, which the link
// makes `env.<name>`), the machine through intrinsics the backend writes in
// place (`__wasm_<name>`): `memory.grow`, `memory.size`, `throw`,
// `memory.copy`, `memory.fill`, the heap's first address, and for a module
// built `--exports` the shadow-stack pointer, the cells' bounds and
// `memory.init`. One thread is
// all there is: a process cell's every access is still atomic, as
// `scripts/rtlint.py` asks of a runtime package.
import mem
import sys
import math
import streams
import stacks
import reset
